---
title: "Get started with bootmakr in Stata"
description: >
  Install the command, run a first bootstrap sensitivity analysis, and read
  its output.
---

```{r helpers, include = FALSE}
source(file.path(getOption("bootmakr.precompute.dir"), "helpers.R"))
st      <- stata_results("first-steps")
first   <- st[st$label == "first", ]
sweep   <- st[st$label == "sweep", ]
program <- st[st$label == "program", ]
```

A regression coefficient from observational data is only as credible as the
assumption that no important variable was left out. A sensitivity analysis
asks how strong an omitted variable would have to be to change the
conclusion. `sensemakr` (Cinelli & Hazlett, 2020) answers with a concrete
number: the coefficient you would have obtained had a variable as strong as
one of your observed covariates been omitted.

`bootmakr` adds inference to that number that does not rely on the
conventional OLS variance formula. It bootstraps the whole procedure, so the
standard error, confidence interval and *p*-value of the adjusted coefficient
remain usable when the errors are heteroskedastic or correlated within
clusters. This page shows a first analysis from start to finish. The same
steps in R are in [Get started in R](bootmakr.html).

## Installation

`bootmakr` builds on the `sensemakr` command, which is on SSC. `bootmakr`
itself is installed from GitHub.

<div class="wrap-code">

```stata
ssc install sensemakr
net install bootmakr, from("https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jespernwulff/bootmakr-stata/main/")
```

</div>

Stata 14 or later is required. To update an existing installation, add the
`replace` option to `net install`. After installation, `help bootmakr` opens
the help file, which is also [on this site](stata-help.html).

## The example

We use the data of Chen, Chittoor, and Vissa (2021), who ask whether CEOs
that belong to the family controlling the firm are paid more than
professional CEOs. The authors share their data on OSF. `copy` downloads the
file to the working directory.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("first-steps", "data", output = FALSE)
```

The data hold 2,011 firm-year observations of 277 Indian firms. `lg_ceopay`
is the logarithm of total CEO pay, `owner_ceo` indicates a family CEO, and
`co_code` identifies the firm. We keep the control variables of the
published model (CEO tenure and education, firm sales and age, ownership
shares, and year and industry indicators) in a global macro.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("first-steps", "controls")
stata_show("first-steps", "regress")
```

This is the published coefficient (Model 2 of their Table 2), with standard
errors clustered by firm. Family CEOs earn more.

## A first analysis

Could an omitted variable explain this away? To answer, `sensemakr` needs a
*benchmark*: an observed covariate that sets the scale for the omitted
variable. We take the age of the firm and ask what the coefficient of
`owner_ceo` would be had a variable *as strong as* `firm_age` been omitted.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("first-steps", "first")
```

The command line has the same form as `regress`: the outcome first, then the
treatment and the covariates. The options name the treatment (`treat()`), the
benchmark (`benchmark()`) and, because observations of the same firm are not
independent, the variable that identifies the clusters to resample
(`cluster()`). Everything else is left at its default: an omitted variable
exactly as strong as the benchmark (`kd(1)`) and 1,000 bootstrap
replications. `seed()` makes the result reproducible. The run takes about
a minute. For longer runs, `dots(100)` prints a dot for every 100
replications.

### Reading the output

**The adjusted estimate.** Had a variable as strong as `firm_age` been
omitted, the coefficient of `owner_ceo` would be `r num(first$estimate, 3)`
rather than 0.327. This is the estimate `sensemakr` reports for the same
benchmark. It does not involve the bootstrap.

**Its uncertainty.** The remaining columns come from the bootstrap. In each
of the 1,000 replications `bootmakr` draws firms with replacement, reruns
`sensemakr` and stores the adjusted estimate. The standard deviation of those
1,000 values is the standard error (`r num(first$se, 3)`), their 2.5th and
97.5th percentiles form the 95 percent confidence interval
(`r num(first$ci_lower, 3)` to `r num(first$ci_upper, 3)`), and the *p*-value
(`r num(first$p, 3, lead = FALSE)`) is twice the smaller of the two shares of
replications that fall on either side of zero. The pay premium of family
CEOs remains positive and statistically distinguishable from zero even if an
omitted variable as strong as firm age is at work.

**The benchmark strength.** The second block says what "as strong as
`firm_age`" means: the share of the remaining variance of the treatment and
of the outcome that firm age explains (partial R^2^), the corresponding
partial correlations, and the strength of the omitted variable these numbers
imply. All of this is computed from the data. No standard error is involved,
so the block is the same whatever variance estimator you prefer. See
[How strong is the benchmark?](benchmark-strength.html) for the details.

### Stored results

`bootmakr` is an r-class command.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("first-steps", "stored")
```

## From one strength to many: the breakdown point

An omitted variable as strong as firm age does not overturn the result. How
much stronger would it have to be? Give `kd()` several values and `bootmakr`
evaluates each of them on the same bootstrap samples. The `plot` option draws
the result.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("first-steps", "sweep")
```

```{r sweep-plot, echo = FALSE, fig.cap = "Adjusted estimate and bootstrap 95 percent confidence interval for an omitted variable 0.5 to 4 times as strong as firm age. Solid markers: p < .05."}
knitr::include_graphics(stata_graph("first-steps-sweep.svg"), error = FALSE)
```

```{r sweep-numbers, include = FALSE}
last_sig  <- max(sweep$kd[sweep$p < 0.05])
first_not <- min(sweep$kd[sweep$p >= 0.05])
```

The adjusted estimate shrinks as the assumed omitted variable grows stronger,
and its confidence interval widens. In this run the result holds up to an
omitted variable `r last_sig` times as strong as firm age and breaks down at
`r first_not` times: from there on the interval includes zero. That
*breakdown point* is a compact summary of how robust the finding is.

A *p*-value close to .05 can fall on either side of it from one seed to the
next when only 1,000 replications are drawn. Before reporting a breakdown
point, increase `reps()` and check that the result has settled. The
[worked example](ceo-pay.html) repeats this sweep with 10,000 replications
and puts the breakdown point at 2.5.

With several values of `kd()`, the full table is returned in `r(results)`
and the implied strength of the omitted variable in `r(benchmark_strength)`.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("first-steps", "sweep-stored")
```

## The choices you make

**The benchmark.** Choose an observed covariate that is strong and that you
can argue an omitted variable is unlikely to exceed. Report why you chose it.
A group of covariates can serve as a joint benchmark with
`gbenchmark(x1 x2)`.

**The multiple.** `kd()` scales the benchmark's relationship with the
treatment and `ky()` its relationship with the outcome. `ky()` follows `kd()`
unless you set it.

**The resampling scheme.** It should mirror how the data were sampled and how
you would compute standard errors in the regression itself. Without
`cluster()`, observations are resampled, which allows for
heteroskedasticity. With `cluster(id)`, whole clusters are resampled, and
`strata()` requests a stratified bootstrap. See
[Clustered and stratified data](clustering.html).

**Replications and seed.** The default of 1,000 replications is fine for a
first look. For results you report, use more (`reps(5000)` or
`reps(10000)`), set a `seed()`, and when the verdict is close to the
significance threshold check that it has settled with the `converge()`
option.

**Many indicator variables.** Stata re-estimates the regression in every
replication. A model with hundreds of indicator variables (say, one per
village or firm) can take about a second per replication, so start such a
model with a few hundred replications and `dots()` to see how long it will
take.

## Bootstrapping your own program

In standard mode `bootmakr` builds the `sensemakr` call for you. If something
has to happen in every replication before `sensemakr` runs, or you want full
control over the call, write a small e-class program and hand it to
`bootmakr` with `program()`. The program must leave `sensemakr`'s
`e(bounds)` behind. `treat()` is then only a label for the output.

```{r, echo = FALSE, results = "asis"}
stata_show("first-steps", "program")
```

```{r program-check, include = FALSE}
same <- isTRUE(all.equal(unlist(first[c("estimate", "se", "ci_lower", "ci_upper", "p")]),
                         unlist(program[c("estimate", "se", "ci_lower", "ci_upper", "p")]),
                         tolerance = 1e-8))
if (!same && !.pending()) stop("program() mode no longer reproduces the first analysis: revise the text below.")
```

With the same seed this reproduces the first analysis exactly. In program
mode the benchmark-strength block shows only the implied strength of the
omitted variable, because `bootmakr` does not know which covariate your
program uses as benchmark.

